From: ypspur_ros2 maintainer <noreply@example.com>
Subject: Workaround for Linux kernel 6.x tcflush behavior (yp-spur issue #245)

Disable serial_flush_out() in encode_write(). Linux kernel 6.x has changed
tcflush(fd, TCOFLUSH) semantics so that the input buffer is also discarded,
which causes odometry_receive_loop() to receive zero bytes and the
coordinator to misbehave / crash under load on Ubuntu 22.04 / 24.04.

Upstream issue: https://github.com/openspur/yp-spur/issues/245
Workaround by: @BND-tc (2025-10-25), confirmed by @yamazak on Ubuntu 24.04.
---
 src/serial.c | 4 +++-
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/serial.c b/src/serial.c
index 6aa5751..8dd7c77 100644
--- a/src/serial.c
+++ b/src/serial.c
@@ -533,7 +533,9 @@ int encode_write(char* data, int len)
   {
     return -1;
   }
-  serial_flush_out();
+  // Workaround for Linux kernel 6.x tcflush behavior (yp-spur issue #245):
+  // tcflush(fd, TCOFLUSH) also flushes the input buffer, breaking odometry.
+  // serial_flush_out();

   return 0;
 }
